[R0295] year=2026 | venue=Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies
TITLE: DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES
TEXT: Information and controlling system The process of managing cybersecurity operations that include continuous monitoring, detection, analysis, and response to cyber threats, aimed at protecting the organization's digital assets, has been investigated in this study. The task addressed is to improve the efficiency and speed of automated incident response by combining deterministic pre-filtering tools with the cognitive capabilities of language models (LLMs). In the course of the research, an architecture of a multi-agent information security management system (MAS-ISM) has been designed, which operates on the basis of decentralized coalition auctions and a hybrid risk analysis subsystem (Sandwich Engine) for express search of incidents in system event logs for automated generation of expert reports in the state language using the generation technology supplemented by Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and LLM search. The solution to the tasks of obtaining technical cybersecurity reports in the state language and recommendations for further decision-making by the analyst has been shown. The main difference of the hybrid risk assessment system from existing ones is the use of deterministic retrospective session isolation, which made it possible to ensure data confidentiality, automatic expert description of the current state of cybersecurity in the national language, and the compilation of recommendations for analysts. The research results are attributed to the combined use of AI methods: RAG technology, deterministic retrospective session isolation, and LLM. The total time for analyzing one complex incident and forming a structured expert SOC report in the state language was reduced to 12 seconds (compared to 15–45 minutes under traditional manual analysis), while guaranteeing the confidentiality of critical data from leakage into the public cloud circuit Keywords: cybersecurity, pre-filtration, LLM, multi-agent systems, event logs, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, hybrid systems UDC 004.056:004.896:510.644 DOI: 10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES Mykhailo Mozhaiev Doctor of Technical Sciences, Senior Researcher, Associate Professor Department of Electronic Computers Kharkiv National University of Radioelectronics Nauky ave., 14, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61166 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1566-9260 Inna Khavina Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1856-1186 Oleksandr Mozhaiev Corresponding author Doctor of Technical Sciences, Professor* E-mail: mozhaev1957@gmail.com ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1412-2696 Volodymyr Strukov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor Department of Mathematical Modeling and Data Analysis V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University Svobody sq., 4, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61022 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4722-3159 Yurii Gnusov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9017-9635 Mykhailo Tsuranov Senior Lecturer* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2115-7029 *Department of Cyber Security and DATA Technologies Kharkiv National University of Internal Affairs L. Landau ave., 27, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61080 Received 12.05.2026 Received in revised form 20.07.2026 Accepted date 29.07.2026 Published date 28.08.2026 How to Cite: Mozhaiev, O., Khavina, I., Strukov, V., Mozhaiev, M., Gnusov, Y., Tsuranov, M. (2026). Development of a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies. Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies, 4 (9 (142)), 33–46. https://doi.org/10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 1. Introduction The modern world is heavily dependent on information technology, which makes cybersecurity extremely important [1]. Various cyberattacks are becoming increasingly complex, threatening both individual users and entire organizations. The current cyberthreat landscape is characterized by a rapid increase in the complexity and intensity of attacks, in particular, zero-day threats and combined fan intrusions. Automated systems for managing information system (IS) events, SIEM systems, are used to solve the task of controlling the flow of events coming from integrated security control systems and to computerize the incident management process. These systems require frequent tuning and adaptation to fully and correctly perform their tasks, as they operate 33 Copyright © 2026 Authors. This is an open access article under the Creative Commons CC BY license Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies ISSN-L 1729-3774; E-ISSN 1729-4061 4/9 ( 142 ) 2026 in a constantly changing, dynamic environment. They collect events in real time, analyze them in near real time, send notifications and display information on the operator console for faster implementation of protective measures. SIEM systems play a critical role in the diverse workflows of analysts at Security Operations Centers (SOCs). However, modern SIEM and AIOps platforms that use machine learning and Big Data to optimize IT operations face a major challenge: they still cannot free analysts from the constant tasks of analyzing and writing technical reports in natural languages and significant incident response times. State-of-the-art LLMs have demonstrated significant capabilities in interpreting unstructured data, generating threat descriptions, and even interacting with security environments through thought chains and augmented search generation. Big language models, using data sets and advanced neural network architectures, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding and generating human language. Thus, the use of big language models (LLMs) fundamentally changes approaches to cybersecurity, allowing for the analysis of unstructured data sets and the interpretation of the context of events directly in real time. However, despite the high analytical potential of LLMs, their direct application in response systems is limited by the high cost of computing resources, the “black box” issue, and the lack of mechanisms for a clear mathematical justification of decisions made (explainability of the result). Therefore, it is urgent to build a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies using a synergy of deterministic analysis and stochastic modeling methods. Such a system could reduce the time for incident analysis, fully ensure data confidentiality, as well as significantly reduce the risk of artificial intelligence errors (hallucinations) when using the state language. The hybrid approach involves the infrastructural and algorithmic combination of the following: – a local contour of the protected perimeter, which, based on deterministic mathematical logic, the TF-IDF algorithm, and finite automata, performs express search, information “noise” filtering, and two-way irreversible anonymization of SIEM system event logs; – an external analytical contour, which uses the cognitive capabilities of Transformer class models exclusively as a linguistic processor to generalize the semantic skeleton of the attack and generate expert reports in the state language in accordance with the requirements of the laws of Ukraine “On ensuring the functioning of the Ukrainian language as the state language” and “On the basic principles of ensuring cybersecurity of Ukraine”. In our work, this approach is implemented by combining the analytical capabilities of neural networks with the decentralized logic of multi-agent systems (MASs) and deterministic mathematical models for choosing optimal protection strategies. 2. Literature review and problem statement The integration of LLM agents into already known systems, including SIEM systems and AI-Ops platforms, is considered in [2]; the work allows us to draw further conclusions about the possibilities of building proactive cybersecurity systems. However, issues such as attacks and interpretability (explainability) of results are still unresolved. At the same time, the direct involvement of LLM in incident response circuits is limited by the significant cost of computing resources, the opacity of decision-making (the “black box” effect), and the lack of tools for their clear mathematical justification. To eliminate these limitations, researchers have turned to multi-agent architectures in the field of cybersecurity. Such systems have decentralized management based on software intelligent agents (SAs), which contain the logic for detecting specific events and each is responsible for analyzing a certain level of threat. This architecture provides scalability, resilience, and parallel processing capabilities, easily integrates into SIEM, and operates in parallel with the system. In [3], the evolution and application of various LLMs are analyzed. The tools used by LLM agents for the cybersecurity industry are considered, such as natural language processing for log analysis, contextual anomaly detection, pattern identification in network traffic, as well as user behavior analysis. It is noted that the use of LLMs is not possible due to data protection and user privacy laws. To work effectively, these systems require high-performance hardware and large memory capacity in terms of RAM and disks, which consumes a lot of energy, which can be an obstacle to the implementation of these systems, especially at small organizations. In [4], a modular multi-agent architecture is described that integrates established cybersecurity analysis tools with large language models (LLMs) to achieve intelligent, understandable, and highly accurate threat detection across a variety of data types. It is found that the underlying framework is a contextual recommendation system that processes and cross-analyzes the outputs of all specialized agents to detect complex threat patterns, such as multi-vector, temporal, or stealth attacks, that would otherwise evade isolated detection mechanisms. The paper does not address the optimality of protection, does not address the issue of state language reporting; data confidentiality may be compromised when using LLMs directly. A review work [5] explores the integration of LLMs into key cybersecurity tasks such as threat detection, and discusses ethical and technical issues, including privacy vulnerabilities, bias, and attacks on LLM applications. The fundamental role of LLM models in improving cybersecurity is emphasized. The lack of practical examples of solving problems is a drawback of the cited work. In [6], a multi-agent network intrusion detection system with a distributed (multi-sensor) architecture is proposed. This framework uses deep anomaly detectors to adapt each agent to changing attack patterns/benign anomalies in its local traffic. A federated learning approach is proposed to share local knowledge between different agents. However, the work does not show a practical solution for creating collective knowledge about attacks based on observations from different sensors. Review [7] examines how LLMs positively impact security and privacy, the potential risks and threats associated with their use, as well as the inherent vulnerabilities in LLMs. It was found that research on the impact of model architecture on the security of LLMs themselves is lacking. This may be due to high computational costs. Today, LLMs do not have direct access to OS and hardware-level functions. Potential threats to LLMs could increase if they were to gain such access. Study [8] highlights the broad potential of LLMs in improving cybersecurity practices. A review of over 150 studies on the application of LLMs to various cybersecurity tasks is given, of which about 35 considered threat detection and 34 Information and controlling system only 6 – log-based anomaly detection. The disadvantage is that they perform semantic analysis on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload. The likely reason is that the capabilities of the LLM model are currently under investigation. Processing performance was increased by using the ForenSift platform developed in [9], which applies retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and LLMs trained on the organization’s datasets for intelligent and adaptive digital evidence processing. It is found that, unlike all traditional log parsing methods, their method is based on clustering and vector embedding to detect anomalies in raw logs but, as practice has shown, the performance of the security system is low. The likely reason is poor clustering of raw logs. Also, parallel processing of streams may not be provided, which could improve response time. For example, paper [10] gives a literature review that discusses in detail the use of LLM for both malware development and detection. It is found that although LLM has great potential, LLM is not a panacea that can prevent or stop every type of malware attack. The disadvantage is that they perform semantic analysis on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload of the model. Study [11] investigates anomaly detection based on system logs, which is a major problem in AIOps as logs provide detailed evidence of failures, performance regressions, and security incidents. It is found that a trained two-layer transformer encoder with masked speech modeling only on regular sessions and anomaly estimation using pseudo-log likelihood is used. But there are drawbacks: external validity (over time, knowledge of the model becomes irrelevant), high cost of inference, heterogeneity of anomalies – over time it is increasingly difficult to detect this method, since the anomaly signal can be diluted by averaging tokens. In [12], the reasoning ability of thirteen LLMs was investigated, which were tested on two datasets of long text analogies. That required ranking a series of stories from most similar to least similar compared to a reference story. The three best LLMs were shown to be GPT-4, GPT-4o, and Gemini, respectively. The drawback was found that LLMs have difficulties with excessive emphasis on similar plot entities (characters and locations) and insufficient awareness of higher-order connections between stories; therefore, to obtain a deterministic answer, the text (raw data) must be filtered. Paper [13] provides information on the automation of report writing, which is a critical aspect of digital forensics investigations. It is found that, in combination with careful proofreading and corrections, LLMs can help practitioners during the report writing process. The disadvantage is the lack of a practical solution, and the work does not consider the generation of reports in the state language. Study [14] describes a decentralized auction-based algorithm for solving dynamic task allocation problems for spatially distributed multi-agent systems. The task allocation is dynamic because it is updated at discrete time steps to take into account the current states of the agents. A greedy coalition auction algorithm is implemented in terms of performance and computation time. The disadvantage of the approach is that when using large MACs, some scalability issues arise due to computation time. Work [15] considers multi-agent cooperative learning systems in which several agents act together to perform a common task. It is found that for the optimal operation of the coalition of agents, a regret minimization algorithm is used. Such an algorithm has not been used in the field of cybersecurity but, given the results reported in the work, it has prospects. In study [16], a solution to the task of optimal robot control is suggested. It was found that the solution was obtained based on a multi-agent system with decentralized control using communication between system agents using an auction protocol. In the field of cybersecurity, such an approach has not been used but, given the results reported in the study, it also has prospects. In [17], to address the challenge of capturing dynamic user behavior and complex interactions in social network content distribution, a method is proposed that captures the spatiotemporal evolution of user behavior, providing a technical approach to personalized recommendations and content resource allocation on social media platforms. The disadvantage is the complexity of applying the method because there are simpler approaches to enriching data on the actions of IS users in space and time. In [18], LLMs with different architectures are investigated, which are tested for their ability to better analyze system log files for security. It was found that the models demonstrate high efficiency in log analysis. The disadvantage is that they require constant fine-tuning, which is especially important for the appropriate adaptation of the domain to certain types of journals. Paper [19] explores the three-way basis of RAG frameworks, which includes search, generation, and addition methods. The disadvantage is the lack of an example of a practical solution. In [20], a report on the shortcomings of RAG systems is given. Seven shortcomings are identified that should be considered when designing the system. The disadvantage of the work is the lack of practical examples of solving problems and the conclusions are of a recommendatory nature. In [21], a modular and extensible agent-based RAG platform is described. It is found that these agents use LLMs as the core of reasoning and planning to determine the flow of program control, while maintaining the characteristics of traditional AI agents. The model evaluates the adequacy of its results and determines whether additional processing is required. However, the effectiveness of CyberRAG directly depends on the performance of the previous intrusion/threat detection system and does not allow for the detection of new types of attacks or attacks that it has not been trained to recognize. Our review of the literature [1–13] allowed us to identify a number of unresolved parts of the general problem that hinder the effective implementation of artificial intelligence in the field of cybersecurity. The general drawback is that semantic analysis is performed either on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload, or on isolated anomalous events, which leads to the loss of the tactical chain of attack. In the above studies on multi-agent systems (MASs), for example [14], it is noted that in large MASs, in which there are more than 10,000 agents, scalability issues arise due to the long calculation time. Methods for managing coalitions of agents are provided in [15–16]. A method for ascertaining the spatial-temporal evolution of user behavior is reported in study [17]. In [18–21], direct transfer of unstructured SIEM log arrays to cloud neural network models leads to an exponen- 35 Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies ISSN-L 1729-3774; E-ISSN 1729-4061 4/9 ( 142 ) 2026 tial increase in the computational complexity of attention mechanisms of Transformer class models, accumulation of errors (AI hallucinations), as well as critical violation of data confidentiality requirements. Thus, analysis of latest scientific publications [1–21] allows us to conclude that despite significant successes in the integration of artificial intelligence technologies in the field of cybersecurity, the problem of effectively combining deterministic pre-filtering tools with the cognitive capabilities of language models remains only partially solved. Most existing solutions are focused on using the stochastic contour of artificial intelligence for both search and event analysis, which leads to a critical overload of the context window of models, the risk of semantic “hallucinations” and the threat of leakage of confidential information. Therefore, there is an objective scientific and practical need to devise a holistic concept that would ensure strict isolation and anonymization of the security context at the local level until the moment of cognitive analysis. This makes it advisable to conduct research aimed at designing a hybrid information security management system capable of minimizing incident response time by means of two-loop deterministic-stochastic processing, while strictly adhering to data confidentiality standards. 3. The aim and objectives of the study The purpose of our research is to design a security system based on multi-agent networks and local RAG models in which a mathematical apparatus is used to filter deterministic entity templates. This could make it possible to increase the efficiency and speed of automated response to cybersecurity incidents. To achieve the goal, the following tasks were set: – to substantiate and formalize the architecture of a multiagent information security management system (MAS-ISM), which operates on the basis of decentralized coalition auctions; – to design a hybrid risk analysis subsystem (Sandwich Engine) for express search of incidents in system event logs and automated generation of expert reports in the state language using LLMs. 4. The study materials and methods The object of our study is the process of managing cybersecurity operations, which includes continuous monitoring, detection, analysis, and response to cyber threats to protect the organization’s digital assets. The principal hypothesis assumes that the local semantic filter reduces the information noise of security logs by compressing the LLM context window. This reduces the load on the context window by hundreds of times and guarantees confidentiality due to local data application. The following assumptions were adopted in the study: – the format and syntactic structure of system logs within one source are conditionally unchanged during one analysis session; – the primary log files entering the input of the local deterministic circuit are authentic and protected from modification by the attacker; – critical incidents and attacker actions have a significantly lower frequency of occurrence in the overall traffic flow than legitimate operations (IDF metric); – the time interval of one user session (for the deterministic retrospective session isolation algorithm) is limited to a fixed window (8–12 hours); – the basic weights and interpretation logic of the gemini-2.5-flash model remain stable, and the generation parameters are minimized (Temperature = 0.0); – an uninterrupted encrypted communication channel is provided between the local development loop and the external API. The following simplifications were accepted: – the local TF-IDF algorithm performs direct tokenization, completely ignoring lemmatization and stemming; – all events within an isolated hacker session have the same mathematical significance for the model without taking into account time decay; – the evolution of the incident is considered within the framework of one compromised account (user_id or IP); – the volume of the initial semantic context for LLM is forcibly limited to the value (N = 6) of the most important relevant events; – experiments were performed on static slices (file samples) of reference datasets. The methodological basis of our study is the following methods: – information retrieval theory and mathematical statistics (TF-IDF algorithm) – for linguistic analysis and statistical weighting of terms in log files; – vector algebra (cosine similarity metrics) – for calculating the degree of semantic proximity and ranking of events in a multidimensional feature space; – graph theory and automaton modeling (regular expressions) – for deterministic entity parsing and retrospective isolation of user sessions; – cryptographic data masking and tokenization – for local anonymization of sensitive data before transferring them to an external analytical cloud loop; – declarative knowledge modeling – for formalizing the system prompt as a tuple of constraints for stochastic generation of large language models; – object-oriented programming and empirical verification – for engineering design of a system prototype and simulation of attacks based on reference data sets. The technology for solving the tasks is based on the justification of a two-circuit deterministic-stochastic architecture and the establishment of regularities of interaction of decentralized multi-agent networks with external generative models (LLMs). The interaction of the circuits is implemented through an intermediate sphere of semantic pre-filtration and deterministic retrospective isolation of sessions, which makes it possible to formalize the system prompt Psys as a declarative model of state space constraints of stochastic AI generation. 5. Research results and analysis of the results related to the multi-agent information security management system 5. 1. Design of the architecture of a multi-agent information security management system We propose the architecture of a multi-agent information security management system (MAS-ISM) for real-time search for optimal solutions for information protection. Fig. 1 shows the MAS-ISM, which is built on the interaction (solid line) in real time of a set of agents responsible for 36 Information and controlling system various protection functions. The functions are implemented { } 3. The set of protection agents A1M ,…, AKM , where K is through their performance in the system and formalized the number of protection mechanisms, determines the pro- in the form of corresponding objective functions and con- tection mechanisms that counteract threats. They determine straints, and determine the optimal action based on auctions the level with which they reduce the existing threat and the carried out through negotiations (dotted line). cost of protection, taking into account the value of the infor- mation being protected at the current time. Threat assessment Resource Agents of defense The knowledge base KB M contains knowledge agents 1 agents mechanisms Auction to enable the operation of a set of resource agents { } A1M ,…, AKM . After the threats and vulnerabilities for the system have been identified and the risks and their priorities have been established, the next stage is to devise an orderly and economically justified protection strategy. .. .. .. Fig. 2 shows options for possible coalitions of agents .. Optimal solution Es = opt(AZi, ARj, AMk,) of protection mechanisms, formed in accordance with the technical capabilities of countering threats acting on resource R2 at the current time, and also structures potential coalitions of protection agents. KВ Z KВ R K KВ M K Symbols attacks; – interaction between agents; – set of threat assessment agents; – set of resource agents; – set of agents of protection mechanisms; KB Z, KB Z, KB Z – knowledge bases of relevant agents auction between protection agents. Еs – optimal solution for the current time. Fig. 1. Architecture of the multi-agent information security management system MAS-ISM Fig. 2. Variants of possible coalitions of agents of protection mechanisms The process of MAS operation for information protection is represented in the following stages: 1. Identification of threats {Z1, …, ZI}, where I is the number of threats that have arrived in a predetermined fixed period of time, is carried out by a set of threat assessment agents { } A1Z,…, AIZ , where I is the number of threats. Identification of threat assessment and vulnerability involves the sequential execution of the following steps: identification of threats to the organization’s resources and determination of the level of threat implementation without the use of protection mechanisms. The knowledge base KB Z contains knowledge to enable the operation of a set of threat assessment agents { } A1Z,…, AIZ . { } 2. A set of resource agents A1R,…, AJR , where J is the number of AS resources, according to the security policy, establishes the appropriate level of confidentiality, integrity, availability, and observability for each resource of the organization and risks and their priorities. The level of confidentiality is determined by the degree of importance of the resource and the consequences of disclosing the relevant information. The level of integrity of the resource is the degree of damage, financial losses, and the possibility of recovery. The level of availability of the resource is the value of the maximum time during which the unavailability of the resource does not negatively affect the activities of the organization, and the level of observability is the degree of completeness, quality, and control of the use of the resource by authorized users. The knowledge base KB R contains knowledge to enable { } the operation of a set of resource agents A1R,…, AJR . To determine the coalition of protection agents K from the set of all possible protection mechanisms { } A1M ,…, AKM , which counteract the threat and assess their level of protection, it is proposed to apply a decentralized auction of agents to find the optimal composition of the set of coalition agents of protection mechanisms according to the criterion of the cost of protection Es and taking into account the value of information, which will allow us to streamline the coordination of protective actions and ensure the stability of the system to the entire spectrum of current vectors ( ) Es = opt AiZ , ARj , AiM , m = 1,…, M, where M is the number of coalitions that were selected for the operation of MAS. Thus, the multi-agent system for information protection has the following structure {{ }} { } { } MAS = A1Z,…, AIZ , A1R ,…, AJR , A1M ,…, AKM , , K1,…, KM , KB1,…, KBm where AZ is the set of threat agents; AR is the set of resource agents; AM is the set of protection mechanism agents; {K1, …, KM} is the set of created coalitions of protection agents during the operation of MAS, in which M is the num- ber of coalitions; {KB1, …, KBm} is the database and knowledge base for the operation of MAS. The interaction of a set of agents responsible for various system functions is implemented through their behavior in 37 Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies ISSN-L 1729-3774; E-ISSN 1729-4061 4/9 ( 142 ) 2026 the system and formalized in the form of corresponding objective functions and constraints. The optimal action of a set of agents based on auctions carried out through negotiations. This will be the optimal solution for the current state of IS. After some time, when the environment (scene) changes, new attacks will be carried out, and the MAS will recalculate a new coalition of protection mechanisms Km according to a similar scenario for the new conditions. The conclusion about the effectiveness of the protection system Es is drawn through the global optimal solution in time and consists of the set of cumulative actions of all coalitions Km ( ) Es = K1 + K2 +,…,+ Km , m = 1,…, M, where M is the number of coalitions that were selected as the best at the current time according to the specified coalition criteria. Experimental verification of the theoretical model of the multi-agent system MAS-ISM was based on assessing the stability of the formation of dynamic coalitions under conditions of multi-vector cyberattacks. The process of a decentralized auction between threat agents and agents of protection mechanisms is formalized as a cooperative game with transferable utility. Not shown in our work. Considering the capabilities and abilities of agents to model the process of detecting, predicting, and defining threats at the enterprise level, the agent can make proactive, rather than reactive, security decisions. This will help activate the necessary data protection tools at the early stages of the information protection process. With this approach, a set of agents operating in real time can resist fan attacks due to the large number of agents and the possibility of their training. 5. 2. Design and examination of the hybrid risk analysis subsystem Sandwich Engine A functional architecture of the hybrid risk analysis subsystem (Sandwich Engine) has been designed for express incident search in system event logs and automated generation of expert reports in the state language using LLM (Fig. 3), in which: 1. The local reprocessing and deterministic analysis module provides parallelization of the processes of primary log parsing (Local Retriever component based on TF-IDF) and contextual enrichment and anonymization of data. This makes it possible to significantly reduce the analysis time as the typing and filtering processes are performed in parallel in separate streams. 2. The local RAG will ensure accuracy and relevance: the critical problem of LLMs is “hallucinations” (the effect of semantic shift or accumulation of statistical error of the stochastic model) and a limited context window. RAG, implemented through TF-IDF and cosine similarity, finds exactly those 5–10 records in millions of log lines that relate to anomalous sessions. LLM receives only concentrated, suppressed information, which guarantees accurate reporting. 3. Synthesis and localization: the LLM neural network takes an isolated part of the log and converts it into structured human text using instructions (System Prompt). The model formats the technical data into an expert report in the state language and immediately generates recommendations for blocking the threat. Raw log stream (SIEM, SOC) 1. Local data preprocessing Component - Retriever Component - IP Enricher 2. Local RAG (TF-IDF Logic) - Attacker session isolation - Background noise suppression (context filtering) 3. Generative AI (Local/API LLM) - Input vector of boundary conditions and instructions - Forming a report in the state language - Expert report: attack timeline, recommendations for eliminating the consequences of the attack Fig. 3. Functional architecture of a hybrid risk analysis subsystem The Retriever component algorithm (Fig. 3): 1. Converts text to numbers and cuts out noise. The log array is broken into lines into individual words (tokens). Using the TF-IDF algorithm (1), it evaluates each word: – ordinary background words that are repeated daily receive zero weight; – rare and dangerous words (for example, credit_card, tunnel, unusual_IP) receive the highest mathematical score. 2. Finds the epicenter of the attack (Geometric search). The Retriever converts each line of the log into a mathematical vector. Then it compares them with the security query vector through cosine similarity (4). Owing to this, the system instantly selects the 6 most important and suspicious lines where an anomaly is recorded. 3. Collects a full history (Session isolation). Instead of simply giving these 6 lines to artificial intelligence (as usual RAG systems in the world do), Retriever takes a unique step: – using regular expressions, it “extracts” the hacker’s name from these anomalous lines (for example, the j_doe account); – then goes back to the general database and retrieves absolutely all actions of this user over the last 8–12 hours. The algorithm of the IP Enricher component (Fig. 3): after the lists of suspicious entities are formed, the local context enrichment procedure is launched, which performs two critical functions before sending the data to the generative model: 1. Network contour typification. Each selected IP address is analyzed by the local ipaddress module. Addresses are classified into internal (RFC 1918) and external: ( ) fnetwork ip = LEAXNTWE/IARNNNTAELRN,AoLthe,irf.ip = PravateRanges, This allows LLM to understand the direction of the attack (external threat or insider). 2. Two-way Anonymization (Data Masking). To guarantee 100% confidentiality, the local enrichment component replaces sensitive data with abstract meta tags in local memory: Mask (ui ) → SUSPECT_USER_Index (ui ), 38 Information and controlling system ( ) ( ) Mask ipj → EXTERNAL_IP_Index ipj . As a result, th